PR #25 now prepares the small checkpoint/demo for transactional publication:

- tracked checkpoint, tokenizer, resolved config, manifest, dataset revision/license, stage history, metrics, limitations, and per-file SHA-256 checksums are bundled;
- CLI and web loading use the artifact manifest rather than a model-name heuristic;
- deterministic generation from the extracted archive passed locally;
- the tag workflow uploads a draft, downloads the actual release assets, validates basename-stable checksums, installs the downloaded wheel into a fresh Python 3.13 environment, verifies the checkpoint, and runs the deterministic sample;
- only after those checks pass does the workflow make the release public.

The remaining acceptance item is external state: merge PR #25 and publish the immutable `v0.1.1` tag/release. Until then, the branch artifact remains explicitly labeled a development preview.

PR #25 now contains every locally implementable v0.1.1 release gate:

- project/runtime versions agree at 0.1.1;
- a tag must point to validated `master` and match the project version;
- package construction happens once on one Ubuntu runner, then the same wheel is install-smoked under Python 3.11, 3.12, and 3.13;
- release notes link issue #8, the fix commit, current GitHub Actions, and Kaggle TPU version 12 evidence;
- checkpoint/config compatibility is documented and covered by tracked restore tests;
- wheel, sdist, deterministic checkpoint archive, basename-stable checksums, reproducible CycloneDX SBOM, and provenance attestations are release assets;
- the GitHub release remains a draft until the uploaded assets are downloaded, checksum-verified, installed in a fresh environment, and used for deterministic checkpoint inference.

The workflow was verified by 338 local non-accelerator tests plus GitHub Linux validation at `85e39f53a24715f641b7aa891529f732897c30e9`. The issue remains open because actually merging and creating `v0.1.1` is a repository release action requiring explicit authorization.
